Dear 3Dprinting.com Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. It is a desktop application that helps
people adapt downloaded 3D-printing models or construct printable parts from
dimensions, sketches and tested components without learning a full CAD system
for every small change.

Here is the most practical example: the downloaded part is almost right, but
its mounting hole is the wrong size. In
Solidon3D 0.3.0, they can click the recognised hole, type a new diameter and
keep the original position and direction—even when the hole is angled.

The change is not painted onto the mesh. It stays in a non-destructive history
and can be edited or undone later. The user can then insert a standards-based
screw hole, heat-set insert or bearing seat and apply fit values measured from
a small printed calibration gauge.

This is part of a much larger development step. Since the first public 0.1
demo, Solidon has recorded 220 curated changes that users can see, including:

* sketches and height previews directly in the 3D view;
* matching printable nuts and screws;
* reusable parametric parts built from history without code;
* real filament spools with colour and print settings kept together;
* 3MF and slicer handoff for the correct material slot;
* Windows, macOS and Linux support.

Solidon runs without an account or cloud project storage. AI chat is optional;
without it, every modelling, analysis and export function remains available.
The complete demo is free until 30 October 2026.

Try it: https://solidon3d.de/en/
See every selected change: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

Reply “demo case” and I will send the build, the intentionally mismatched
STL, the corrected project and ready-to-publish images. The whole example can
be reproduced in a few minutes.

